<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.1.2: Writing and Graphing Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00370</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.1.2: Writing and Graphing Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a2309f6f-0e22-48bb-85fe-96a673f0fff9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Use the situation you saw earlier to answer 1 - 3.</para>
      <para>
        <quote>Diego bought some raisins and walnuts to make trail mix. Raisins cost $4 a pound and walnuts cost $8 a pound. Diego spent $15 on both ingredients..</quote>
     </para>
      <!--Q1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Write an equation to represent this constraint. Let \(x\) be the pounds of raisins and \(y\) be the pounds of
        walnuts.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>4x+8y=15.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q2-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph your equation from question 2 that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below. 
 </item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/4bbd802f88f57dc53c3ba0534fb1a8e09b127198.html" width="500" height="400"/><example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="Graph of a line. walnuts (pounds), raisin (pounds).">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_03_03_01.svg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q3-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para> Complete the table with the amount of one ingredient Diego could have bought given the other. Be prepared to
        show your reasoning.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Raisins (lbs)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Walnuts (lbs)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>0.25</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry/>
                  <entry>1.375</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry/>
                  <entry>1.25</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>1.75</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>When you have filled out the table, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Raisins (lbs)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Walnuts (lbs)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>1.875</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>0.25</entry>
                  <entry>1.75</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>1.375</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>1.25</entry>
                  <entry>1.25</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>1.75</entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>0.375</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>Use the following new piece of information to answer questions 4 - 6.</para>
      <para>Diego bought a total of 2 pounds of raisins and walnuts combined.</para>
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise>
        <div class="os-raise-ib-content" data-schema-version="1.0" data-wait-for-event="Reveal4">
  </div>
        <problem>
          <para> Write an equation to represent this constraint. Let \(x\) be the pounds of raisins and \(y\) be the pounds of
        walnuts.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x+y=2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph your equation from question 5 that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below. </item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/4bbd802f88f57dc53c3ba0534fb1a8e09b127198.html" width="500" height="400"/><example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="Graph of a line. walnuts (pounds), raisin (pounds).">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_03_03_02.svg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q6-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para> On your own paper, complete the table with the amount of one ingredient Diego could have bought given the other. Be prepared to
        show your reasoning.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Raisins (lbs)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Walnuts (lbs)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>0.25</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry/>
                  <entry>1.375</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry/>
                  <entry>1.25</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>1.75</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry/>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>When you have filled out the table, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Raisins (lbs)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Walnuts (lbs)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>0</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>0.25</entry>
                  <entry>1.75</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>0.625</entry>
                  <entry>1.375</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>0.75</entry>
                  <entry>1.25</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>1.75</entry>
                  <entry>0.25</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>-1 (not possible)</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>Use the following information to answer 7 - 9.</para>
      <para>Diego spent $15 and bought exactly 2 pounds of raisins and walnuts.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="b05d3cc6-ea6f-494b-8192-510375b2ec87" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para> How many pounds of raisins did he buy?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 0.25</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 0.25.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_9f48d8a8-676f-403a-a44b-ed25865eda77" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para> How many pounds of walnuts did he buy?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 1.75</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 1.75.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q9-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Explain or show how you know how many pounds he purchased of each type.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here are samples.</para>
          <list>
            <item> It’s the only pair of values that appears in both tables.</item>
            <item> If the two equations are graphed on the same coordinate plane, it’s where the two graphs intersect, so the pair of values meets both requirements.</item>
          </list>
          <figure>
            <media alt="Graph of a linear system. walnuts (pounds), raisin (pounds).">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_03_03_03.svg"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>